CHAPTER IV
VIOLENCE
Article 14
Violence against women consists of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; exploitation; and/or
e. other violence;

CHAPTER III
SCOPE OF PROTECTION
Article 4
The Scope of Implementation of Protection for Women victims of violence includes:
a. prevention;
b. service/handling;
c. empowerment;

Article 17...
Article 17
Sexual violence as referred to in Article 14 letter c includes sexual violence as meant in the form of sexual harassment:
a. acts that are inappropriate or unwanted;
b. forced sexual relations with and/or others for commercial purposes;
c. forced sexual relations with persons and/or for certain purposes.

BAB II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The implementation of Protection for Women victims of violence is based on: a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment

Article 24
The community and family have the obligation and responsibility to:
a. prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women;
b. provide information and/or report acts of violence against women to the competent authorities;
c. provide protection to victims;
d. provide emergency assistance;
e. participate in the handling of victims of violence:

Article 3
The implementation of Protection for Women victims of violence aims to: a. prevent violence against women;
b. provide services/handling to victims; and
c. carry out empowerment for women victims of violence and vulnerable groups;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women.

Article 5
(1) Efforts to prevent violence against women shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the SKPD responsible for Women's Empowerment;
(2) Prevention Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:
a. establishing a working network;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a violence prevention system;
d. disseminating laws and regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence;
e. providing education for women victims of violence;
f. through Communication, Information and Education (KIE) media.

CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 21
Every woman victim of violence has the right:
a. to have her dignity and worth as a human being respected;
b. to obtain physical, psychological and social health recovery in accordance with the suffering experienced by the victim of violence;
c. to obtain protection from family, community, Government, Regional Government and/or other parties, either temporarily or based on a Court Determination;
d. special handling accompanied by confidentiality of the victim;
e. accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to spiritual guidance services;
g. to determine her own decision;

Article 22
(1) The Regional Government is obligated to carry out, provide, and be responsible for protection efforts for women victims of violence in the form of:
a. establishing PPT;
b. policy formulation;
c. preparing plans and implementing programs and activities;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure; and
e. supervising the provision of services to victims in accordance with minimum service standards.
(2) In implementing the obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall prepare a Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women.
(3) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by a Governor Regulation.

Article 29
(1) The community participates in the implementation of protection by:
a. forming family partners at the kelurahan/community level and within community organizations;
b. forming community women's protection units;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's rights;
d. undertaking preventive measures if indications of violence against women are found in the community;
e. providing first aid to victims; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against women occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, community social institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and/or mass media.

